          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes affects blood sugar primarily due to insulin resistance and inadequate insulin production. In this condition, the body's cells become less sensitive to insulin, the hormone that helps regulate blood sugar levels. As a result, glucose cannot enter the cells effectively, leading to elevated levels of sugar in the bloodstream.\n\nAdditionally, over time, the pancreas may not produce enough insulin to meet the body's needs, further contributing to high blood sugar levels. Lifestyle factors, such as diet and physical activity, can also play a significant role in managing blood sugar levels in individuals with type 2 diabetes.\n\nI could not cover the detailed biochemical processes involved in insulin resistance or the specific long-term effects of type 2 diabetes on the body.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
